00:54:11.540 --> 00:54:14.540
After that, what do you do?

00:54:14.540 --> 00:54:19.540
You apply tracing and observability on your application.

00:54:19.540 --> 00:54:27.540
If I explain tracing in simple words,

00:54:27.540 --> 00:54:39.540
so tracing is nothing but following your query throughout the workflow.

00:54:39.540 --> 00:54:44.540
I explain it in simple words.

00:54:44.540 --> 00:54:47.540
What happens in a Rack application?

00:54:47.540 --> 00:54:54.540
Suppose my student asks a question from my chatbot.

00:54:54.540 --> 00:54:57.540
Where will that question go first?

00:54:57.540 --> 00:54:59.540
It will go to my retriever.

00:54:59.540 --> 00:55:09.540
My retriever will take this question and retrieve some chunks from the vector database.

00:55:09.540 --> 00:55:16.540
Now I will pass these query and chunks to the augmentation step.

00:55:16.540 --> 00:55:21.540
Where I will combine them to a prompt.

00:55:21.540 --> 00:55:28.540
So in this prompt, I will add my system prompt, chunks and query.

00:55:28.540 --> 00:55:33.540
After that, I will give this prompt to my LM.

00:55:33.540 --> 00:55:41.540
And my system prompt will read the LM, retrieved context, query and on this basis,

00:55:41.540 --> 00:55:44.540
it will generate a response.

00:55:44.540 --> 00:55:48.540
So the journey from query to response,

00:55:48.540 --> 00:55:52.540
that the query went to the retriever, what did the retriever do,

00:55:52.540 --> 00:55:56.540
what chunks did the retriever retrieve, how did they get augmented in the prompt,

00:55:56.540 --> 00:56:00.540
how did the prompt go to the LM, what did the LM respond to.

00:56:00.540 --> 00:56:06.540
So this whole journey that you are tracking is called tracing.

00:56:06.540 --> 00:56:14.540
And through this, you can track the journey from your application of every query.

00:56:14.540 --> 00:56:16.540
Now what is the benefit of this?

00:56:16.540 --> 00:56:21.540
That during tracing, your application not only locks this journey,

00:56:21.540 --> 00:56:25.540
but also locks that in this whole step,

00:56:25.540 --> 00:56:30.540
that is, how much latency was taken from query to response,

00:56:30.540 --> 00:56:32.540
that is, how much time was taken,

00:56:32.540 --> 00:56:36.540
that if you gave input, then how much time did you spend on output,

00:56:36.540 --> 00:56:39.540
then you can calculate the average latency here.

00:56:39.540 --> 00:56:45.540
The second benefit here is that you can also calculate the average cost here.

00:56:45.540 --> 00:56:48.540
That in processing every query,

00:56:48.540 --> 00:56:52.540
till the response, how much money you spent per API call,

00:56:52.540 --> 00:56:55.540
you can also calculate all these things.

00:56:55.540 --> 00:56:57.540
So what is the benefit of tracing?

00:56:57.540 --> 00:57:02.540
That it basically tells you the internals of your application,

00:57:02.540 --> 00:57:07.540
that for a given question, how your application behaves internally.

00:57:07.540 --> 00:57:09.540
And what is observability?

00:57:09.540 --> 00:57:13.540
In observability, the traces that you have collected,

00:57:13.540 --> 00:57:17.540
from inside them, you calculate the aggregate metrics,

00:57:17.540 --> 00:57:20.540
as I told you, average latency, average cost,

00:57:20.540 --> 00:57:24.540
and you visualize them through dashboards.

00:57:24.540 --> 00:57:26.540
So this is your observability.

00:57:26.540 --> 00:57:28.540
Tracing is collecting the logs,

00:57:28.540 --> 00:57:32.540
observability is visualizing them through dashboards.

00:57:32.540 --> 00:57:33.540
That's it.

00:57:33.540 --> 00:57:38.540
Now what is your application here is ready.

00:57:38.540 --> 00:57:44.540
But the problem is that your application,

00:57:45.540 --> 00:57:49.540
the chatbot of the campus, it is not secured.

00:57:52.540 --> 00:57:54.540
So what will you do in the next step?

00:57:54.540 --> 00:58:00.540
Around your application, you will build a security layer.

00:58:02.540 --> 00:58:07.540
And this security layer is called your guardrails.

00:58:09.540 --> 00:58:11.540
If I tell you about guardrails,

00:58:11.540 --> 00:58:15.540
then guardrails are simply a wrapper, a layer around your application.

00:58:15.540 --> 00:58:19.540
So anytime you see that your application,

00:58:19.540 --> 00:58:21.540
guardrails are placed around it.

00:58:21.540 --> 00:58:23.540
Means if you have an application,

00:58:23.540 --> 00:58:28.540
then guardrails are placed around it in this way.

00:58:28.540 --> 00:58:31.540
It acts as a fencing to your application.

00:58:31.540 --> 00:58:38.540
And this safeguards your application from attacks.

00:58:39.540 --> 00:58:41.540
Like I give you some examples.

00:58:41.540 --> 00:58:46.540
Like a student came to your campus chatbot

00:58:46.540 --> 00:58:53.540
and he is querying about a course that he has not purchased.

00:58:53.540 --> 00:58:58.540
So would you allow that student to ask this query

00:58:58.540 --> 00:59:01.540
or will you give the answer to that query?

00:59:01.540 --> 00:59:02.540
The answer is no.

00:59:02.540 --> 00:59:07.540
You will decline that query by saying that you have not purchased this course.

00:59:07.540 --> 00:59:09.540
So I cannot give you this answer.

00:59:09.540 --> 00:59:11.540
So this is kind of a safeguard.

00:59:11.540 --> 00:59:14.540
This is kind of a guardrail job that you will put on your application.

00:59:14.540 --> 00:59:18.540
Let's say a student is abusing my chatbot.

00:59:18.540 --> 00:59:22.540
Would you allow that type of query to pass through the application?

00:59:22.540 --> 00:59:23.540
The answer is no.

00:59:23.540 --> 00:59:25.540
You will not allow this.

00:59:25.540 --> 00:59:29.540
Now our many courses are in live setting.

00:59:29.540 --> 00:59:32.540
Like this course is also in live setting.

00:59:32.540 --> 00:59:36.540
So generally what happens is that we interact with the students.

00:59:36.540 --> 00:59:40.540
So the questions that are asked in the chat and we give them the answer.

00:59:40.540 --> 00:59:45.540
So many times while giving the answer, we refer any student to his name.

00:59:45.540 --> 00:59:47.540
Like someone asked a question.

00:59:47.540 --> 00:59:49.540
Let's say there is a student Amit.

00:59:49.540 --> 00:59:53.540
If he asked me a question, I would say that the answer to your question is this.

00:59:53.540 --> 01:00:00.540
So we don't want that when any of my students is querying from this chatbot,

01:00:00.540 --> 01:00:03.540
then there is the name of Amit or any other student.

01:00:03.540 --> 01:00:04.540
So there is a name of Amit.

01:00:04.540 --> 01:00:07.540
That is a private information to my student.

01:00:07.540 --> 01:00:09.540
This is his personal private information.

01:00:09.540 --> 01:00:16.540
So what I will do is I will put a guardrail here so that no personal information in the output.

01:00:16.540 --> 01:00:20.540
My chatbot doesn't give it.

01:00:20.540 --> 01:00:23.540
It is called PII.

01:00:23.540 --> 01:00:27.540
So I will set a guardrail around it.

01:00:27.540 --> 01:00:29.540
So what is basically a guardrail?

01:00:29.540 --> 01:00:35.540
Basically it is a wrapper around your application that makes your application secure around attacks

01:00:35.540 --> 01:00:40.540
and unauthorized or unauthenticated queries are blocked.

01:00:40.540 --> 01:00:43.540
I hope this point is clear to you.

01:00:43.540 --> 01:00:46.540
So we built our application here.

01:00:46.540 --> 01:00:52.540
Through experiment tracking, we got our best combination of parameters.

01:00:54.540 --> 01:00:58.540
After that, we added tracing and observability here.

01:00:58.540 --> 01:01:03.540
And finally, we made our application secure through guardrails.

01:01:03.540 --> 01:01:07.540
Now what I will do here using fast API.

01:01:07.540 --> 01:01:10.540
I will serve my application.

01:01:10.540 --> 01:01:13.540
How is our application now?

01:01:16.540 --> 01:01:19.540
Our application is written in line chain and line graph.

01:01:19.540 --> 01:01:21.540
It is a backend.

01:01:21.540 --> 01:01:25.540
Now how to execute this backend code?

01:01:25.540 --> 01:01:27.540
I know this as a developer.

01:01:27.540 --> 01:01:37.540
But it is possible that my front-end application is written in a language that cannot run this backend code directly.

01:01:37.540 --> 01:01:41.540
Like my backend is written in Python because I am using line chain.

01:01:41.540 --> 01:01:44.540
It is possible that my front-end is written in JavaScript.

01:01:44.540 --> 01:01:50.540
So this front-end cannot run my Python code directly because there is a mismatch of languages.

01:01:50.540 --> 01:01:53.540
So what to do here?

01:01:53.540 --> 01:01:58.540
What I will do here is I will build APIs around my backend.

01:01:58.540 --> 01:02:00.540
What are APIs?

01:02:00.540 --> 01:02:02.540
APIs are basically your middleman.

01:02:02.540 --> 01:02:05.540
They act as your middleman.

01:02:05.540 --> 01:02:09.540
Now instead of contacting your front-end directly from the backend,

01:02:09.540 --> 01:02:11.540
What will it do?

01:02:11.540 --> 01:02:13.540
It will contact this middleman.

01:02:13.540 --> 01:02:15.540
And how will this contact happen?

01:02:15.540 --> 01:02:17.540
Through the HTTP protocol.

01:02:17.540 --> 01:02:21.540
So this contact will be done through the HTTP protocol.

01:02:21.540 --> 01:02:26.540
And now my front-end will send some requests through the HTTP methods.

01:02:26.540 --> 01:02:32.540
And my API will send a response to my front-end.

01:02:32.540 --> 01:02:37.540
And these APIs will be connected to my backend.

01:02:37.540 --> 01:02:42.540
So they will act as a middleman between my front-end and backend.

01:02:42.540 --> 01:02:50.540
You can understand this through an example that my backend is a kitchen in a restaurant.

01:02:50.540 --> 01:02:53.540
Now your food is made in the kitchen.

01:02:53.540 --> 01:02:55.540
The chef makes the food.

01:02:55.540 --> 01:02:59.540
Front-end is the customer.

01:02:59.540 --> 01:03:03.540
The customer comes to my restaurant and has to order some dishes.

01:03:03.540 --> 01:03:06.540
So he does not place his order directly in the kitchen.

01:03:06.540 --> 01:03:08.540
What does my customer do here?

01:03:08.540 --> 01:03:13.540
The waiter, which is the middleman, gives his order to the waiter.

01:03:13.540 --> 01:03:16.540
This waiter takes my order to the kitchen.

01:03:16.540 --> 01:03:20.540
And the ready dish is served to my customer.

01:03:20.540 --> 01:03:24.540
So this is what we do here through the APIs.

01:03:24.540 --> 01:03:27.540
And this is called application serving.

01:03:30.540 --> 01:03:35.540
And the APIs that you build in your JNI applications,

01:03:35.540 --> 01:03:39.540
you keep streaming endpoints in them.

01:03:39.540 --> 01:03:41.540
What does this mean?

01:03:41.540 --> 01:03:45.540
Whatever response you get, it is received in a streaming fashion on your front-end.

01:03:45.540 --> 01:03:51.540
Like you must have seen in the chat GPT, if you do a query, you get the answer printed there.

01:03:51.540 --> 01:03:59.540
So here too you get the answer printed by token by token.

01:03:59.540 --> 01:04:02.540
I hope this point is clear to you.

01:04:02.540 --> 01:04:11.540
So here we have finally served our application, which is our backend, through the fast API.

01:04:11.540 --> 01:04:15.540
Now our deployment step has started.

01:04:15.540 --> 01:04:17.540
So what do you do next?

01:04:17.540 --> 01:04:24.540
Next your deployment starts and in deployment, the next step is you package your application.

01:04:24.540 --> 01:04:29.540
What do you do?

01:04:29.540 --> 01:04:33.540
You containerize your application using docker.

01:04:33.540 --> 01:04:37.540
So your application is not just a single application.

01:04:37.540 --> 01:04:43.540
There are some other things attached to your application, like its environment.

01:04:43.540 --> 01:04:51.540
The environment that you have made through UV or VNV, and the dependencies related to it.

01:04:51.540 --> 01:05:07.540
So what you do in containerization, you package all these things, your app, its environment and related dependencies in a container.

01:05:07.540 --> 01:05:18.540
And what you can simply do is you take this container anywhere, you deploy it somewhere and you simply run this package.

01:05:18.540 --> 01:05:30.540
And as soon as you run this package, your application will work on your deployed infrastructure as it is working on your laptop.

01:05:30.540 --> 01:05:39.540
So you package your application, its environment, dependencies, and then you take it to the infrastructure where it has to be deployed.

01:05:39.540 --> 01:05:46.540
So if you simply run it, then your application will work exactly as it is working on your laptop.

01:05:46.540 --> 01:05:50.540
And you will not face any dependency related issues.

01:05:50.540 --> 01:05:54.540
This is called your containerization and packaging.

01:05:54.540 --> 01:05:59.540
That is the next step in our LLM Ops lifecycle.

01:05:59.540 --> 01:06:01.540
Then comes the next step around CICD.

01:06:01.540 --> 01:06:08.540
What you do in CICD is basically you are deploying a Rack application here.

01:06:08.540 --> 01:06:12.540
It may be possible that you deploy another version of your application tomorrow.

01:06:12.540 --> 01:06:22.540
So what you do in CICD is you automate the testing and deployment.

01:06:22.540 --> 01:06:31.540
What you do in CICD is you automate your testing.

01:06:31.540 --> 01:06:37.540
You have the V2 of the application, the first thing you will do is you will test it against V1.

01:06:37.540 --> 01:06:41.540
You will check that this V2 version is better than my V1 or not.

01:06:41.540 --> 01:06:43.540
If it is better, then you will deploy it.

01:06:43.540 --> 01:06:45.540
If it is worse, then why will you deploy it?

01:06:45.540 --> 01:06:47.540
You will stay on V1.

01:06:47.540 --> 01:06:52.540
If it is better, then you automatically test it through the concept of CID.

01:06:52.540 --> 01:07:01.540
And then what you do through the concept of CID is if this V2 version is better than your V1, then you package it.

01:07:01.540 --> 01:07:06.540
So CID is nothing but your automated packaging.

01:07:06.540 --> 01:07:12.540
Now what will happen after completing this CID step?

01:07:12.540 --> 01:07:15.540
You will have just a second.

01:07:15.540 --> 01:07:21.540
You will have a packaged version 2 of your application.

01:07:21.540 --> 01:07:26.540
Packaged V2 of your application.

01:07:26.540 --> 01:07:30.540
It will be ready with you.

01:07:30.540 --> 01:07:33.540
Now what is the next step?

01:07:33.540 --> 01:07:39.540
The next step is you have to deploy it on a cloud provider.

01:07:39.540 --> 01:07:41.540
The next step is your cloud deployment.

01:07:41.540 --> 01:07:53.540
You can use any cloud platform, whether it is AWS, Microsoft Azure, Google GCP.

01:07:53.540 --> 01:07:56.540
You go there and deploy it.

01:07:56.540 --> 01:07:59.540
You take this packaged version on your cloud infrastructure.

01:07:59.540 --> 01:08:02.540
You run it there and your application is live.

01:08:02.540 --> 01:08:06.540
I mean to say that your APIs are live.

01:08:06.540 --> 01:08:11.540
Now your front end can interact with these APIs and use your application.

01:08:11.540 --> 01:08:14.540
But the story does not end on deployment.

01:08:14.540 --> 01:08:20.540
There are more steps that you do in this LLM ops lifecycle.

01:08:20.540 --> 01:08:25.540
You also execute post deployment steps here.

01:08:26.540 --> 01:08:31.540
So what do you mean by post deployment?

01:08:31.540 --> 01:08:39.540
First of all, after deployment, you apply tracing and observability for your user queries.

01:08:39.540 --> 01:08:46.540
Now this chatbot that you made, the campus chatbot, has been deployed and integrated with your website.

01:08:46.540 --> 01:08:52.540
So now the queries that will come to your user, you will implement tracing on them.

01:08:52.540 --> 01:09:01.540
Basically, you will track the queries of every student in your application and lock them.

01:09:01.540 --> 01:09:07.540
So that you know how your users are interacting with your application.

01:09:07.540 --> 01:09:09.540
What kind of questions they are giving you.

01:09:09.540 --> 01:09:11.540
You will have all this knowledge.

01:09:11.540 --> 01:09:14.540
And what can you do in the future?

01:09:14.540 --> 01:09:18.540
You can run online evals on your user queries.

01:09:18.540 --> 01:09:24.540
And you can evaluate your application after deployment.

01:09:24.540 --> 01:09:27.540
Whether it is working as intended or not.

01:09:27.540 --> 01:09:29.540
Has there been any drift in it?

01:09:29.540 --> 01:09:32.540
Has the score not decreased after deployment?

01:09:32.540 --> 01:09:35.540
So you can monitor all these things.

01:09:35.540 --> 01:09:41.540
And not only do you monitor the application here through tracing, observability and online evals.

01:09:41.540 --> 01:09:45.540
You also monitor your infrastructure here.

01:09:45.540 --> 01:09:51.540
On the infrastructure, let's say if I talk about AWS, then there are EC2 machines.

01:09:51.540 --> 01:09:55.540
This is your compute infrastructure where your application is live.

01:09:55.540 --> 01:10:00.540
So not only do you monitor your application and its health.

01:10:00.540 --> 01:10:06.540
After deployment, you also monitor the health of your infrastructure and its health metrics.

01:10:06.540 --> 01:10:08.540
You also monitor the health of your application.

01:10:08.540 --> 01:10:11.540
Like CPU usage, network usage, RAM usage, disk usage.

01:10:11.540 --> 01:10:14.540
You monitor all these things.

01:10:14.540 --> 01:10:20.540
And after this, your entire LLM Ops lifecycle is completed.

